### 2.4 `minix/ipc.h`

**C11 keywords, not GNU attributes.** `_Alignas`, `_Alignof` and `_Static_assert` are C11 *keywords* — no header needed, guaranteed under `-std=c11 -pedantic-errors`, and portable to any conforming C11 compiler. `__attribute__((aligned(8)))` / `__alignof__` are clang/gcc extensions that `-std=c11` does not guarantee and `-pedantic` may flag. The only include is `<stddef.h>` for `offsetof`, which C11 §4p6 lists among the freestanding headers — so it comes from clang's resource dir and survives `-nostdlibinc`.

One subtlety: C11 does not permit an alignment specifier on a struct *type* declaration. Put `_Alignas(8)` on the **first member**; the struct's alignment is the max of its members', so this yields `_Alignof(message) == 8`, and `4 + 4 + 96 = 104` is already a multiple of 8.

```c
#ifndef _MINIX_IPC_H
#define _MINIX_IPC_H

#include <stddef.h>   /* offsetof -- a freestanding header (C11 4p6) */

/* ---- endpoints (kernel-shared/src/endpoint.rs) ---- */
typedef int endpoint_t;
_Static_assert(sizeof(int) == 4, "minix.rs ABI assumes a 32-bit int");

#define ENDPOINT_GEN_SHIFT      15
#define ENDPOINT_PROC_MASK      0x7fff
#define ENDPOINT_MAX_GENERATION 65535
#define ENDPOINT_SLOT_TOP       16383

/*
 * Endpoint packing. minix.rs deviates from MINIX 3 deliberately: the proc
 * field is SIGN-EXTENDED from bit ENDPOINT_GEN_SHIFT-1 to recover negative
 * task slots, rather than MINIX 3's MAX_NR_TASKS offset bias. Mirrors
 * endpoint.rs::{make_endpoint, endpoint_proc, endpoint_gen}. The consequence
 * is _ENDPOINT(0, p) != p for negative p -- see the _EP macros in minix/com.h.
 */
#define _ENDPOINT(g, p)  (((g) << ENDPOINT_GEN_SHIFT) | ((p) & ENDPOINT_PROC_MASK))
#define _ENDPOINT_G(e)   ((e) >> ENDPOINT_GEN_SHIFT)
#define _ENDPOINT_P(e)                                          \
    ((((e) & ENDPOINT_PROC_MASK) & (1 << (ENDPOINT_GEN_SHIFT - 1)))  \
        ? (((e) & ENDPOINT_PROC_MASK) | ~ENDPOINT_PROC_MASK)         \
        : ((e) & ENDPOINT_PROC_MASK))

/* ---- message (kernel-shared/src/message.rs) ---- */
struct message {
    _Alignas(8) endpoint_t m_source;   /* set by the kernel on delivery  */
    int                    m_type;     /* call number out, result back   */
    unsigned char          payload[96];
};
typedef struct message message;

_Static_assert(sizeof(message) == 104,           "message size drifted from Message");
_Static_assert(_Alignof(message) == 8,           "message alignment drifted");
_Static_assert(offsetof(message, m_source) == 0, "m_source offset drifted");
_Static_assert(offsetof(message, m_type) == 4,   "m_type offset drifted");
_Static_assert(offsetof(message, payload) == 8,  "payload offset drifted");

/* ---- IPC primitives (kernel-shared/src/ipc_const.rs) ---- */
#define SEND            1
#define RECEIVE         2
#define SENDREC         3
#define NOTIFY          4
#define SENDNB          5
#define SENDA           16
#define MINIX_KERNINFO  32
#define NOTIFY_MESSAGE  0x1000

#endif /* _MINIX_IPC_H */
```

The 104/8/0/4/8 numbers are `format!`ed from `size_of::<Message>()` / `align_of::<Message>()` / live pointer arithmetic — never typed in.

**Deliberately absent:** any statement about which register carries the primitive number. `ipc_const.rs:3`'s "`r1`/`x16`" is wrong (`minix-ipc/src/lib.rs:11` and `user_stub.S` confirm `x1`), and the phase-5 plan assigns that fix to **slice 5.1**. The generator sidesteps the conflict by citing the module instead of repeating the claim — flagged below as a decision worth a second opinion.

### 2.5 `minix/com.h`

The high-value content here is the `_PROC_NR` vs `_EP` split, because `boot_endpoint(p) != p` for negative `p` is exactly the footgun a generated header should eliminate.

```c
#include <minix/ipc.h>   /* endpoint_t and the _ENDPOINT* packing macros */

#define NR_TASKS       5
#define NR_PROCS       1024
#define NR_SYS_PROCS   64
#define NR_BOOT_PROCS  11

#define ANY   ((endpoint_t) 16383)
#define NONE  ((endpoint_t) 16382)
#define SELF  ((endpoint_t) 16381)

#define OK 0     /* kernel-shared/src/error.rs; MINIX 3 keeps this in const.h */

/*
 * Every process below has TWO macros, and they are NOT interchangeable:
 *
 *   <NAME>_PROC_NR   the process-table slot (NEGATIVE for kernel tasks)
 *   <NAME>_EP        the boot endpoint -- what belongs in an IPC call
 *
 * They differ for tasks because minix.rs sign-extends the endpoint proc field
 * instead of using MINIX 3's offset bias (see minix/ipc.h). Naming a task by
 * its _PROC_NR in an IPC call is a bug.
 */
#define ASYNCM_PROC_NR    (-5)
#define ASYNCM_EP         ((endpoint_t) 32763)
#define IDLE_PROC_NR      (-4)
#define IDLE_EP           ((endpoint_t) 32764)
#define CLOCK_PROC_NR     (-3)
#define CLOCK_EP          ((endpoint_t) 32765)
#define SYSTEM_PROC_NR    (-2)
#define SYSTEM_EP         ((endpoint_t) 32766)
#define HARDWARE_PROC_NR  (-1)
#define HARDWARE_EP       ((endpoint_t) 32767)

#define PM_PROC_NR   0
#define PM_EP        ((endpoint_t) 0)
/* … VFS 1, RS 2, MEM 3, TTY 4, DS 5, MFS 6, VM 7, PFS 8, SCHED 9, INIT 10 … */

#define EXEC_ONLY_PROC_NR (-1)

/* The C macros must reproduce the Rust encode/decode exactly. These fire in
   every TU that includes this header, with no libc and no sysroot. */
_Static_assert(SYSTEM_EP == _ENDPOINT(0, SYSTEM_PROC_NR), "…");
_Static_assert(_ENDPOINT_P(SYSTEM_EP) == SYSTEM_PROC_NR,  "…");
_Static_assert(_ENDPOINT_G(SYSTEM_EP) == 0,               "…");
_Static_assert(_ENDPOINT_P(HARDWARE_EP) == HARDWARE_PROC_NR, "…");
_Static_assert(_ENDPOINT_P(PM_EP) == PM_PROC_NR,          "…");
_Static_assert(_ENDPOINT_P(INIT_EP) == INIT_PROC_NR,      "…");
```

Those endpoint asserts are the best value-per-line in the whole slice: they validate the hand-written C decode macro against Rust-computed constants at compile time, on any host, with no libc.

The `_EP` values come from calling `com::boot_endpoint(nr)` in the generator (no such `pub const Endpoint` exists in Rust today; do **not** add one — the generator's job is exactly to derive it).

**Deliberate omissions**, each with a one-line comment in the header so the choice is visible and reversible: `NR_STUB_PROCS` / `STUB_*_PROC_NR` (demo-only, cargo-feature-scoped, no C consumer), `NR_SERVED_PROCS` (server-table sizing, no C consumer), `USER_VA_TOP` (kernel-internal), `sys_limits::*` (priv-slot caps), and signal numbers (musl's `<signal.h>` is authoritative and already agrees on SIGKILL 9 / SIGSEGV 11 / SIGTERM 15).

### 2.6 `minix/callnr.h`

`#include <minix/ipc.h>` (needs `NOTIFY_MESSAGE` for the ordering asserts). Sections, all hex except the counts and small flags:

- `KERNEL_CALL 0x600` + `SYS_GETINFO … SYS_ENDKSIG` (`0x600`–`0x611`)
- `NR_KERN_CALLS 18`, `NR_SYS_CALLS 32`
- `GET_WHOAMI 12`, `SYS_GETINFO_NAME_LEN 16`, `EXEC_NAME_LEN 16`
- `PRIVCTL_SET_USER 1`, `SCHEDCTL_FLAG_KERNEL 1`
- `VMCTL_PT_MAP … VMCTL_VMINHIBIT_CLEAR` (1–6), `NR_VMCTL_SUBCALLS 6`, `VMCTL_PROT_WRITE 1`, `VMCTL_PROT_EXEC 2`
- the five bands: `PM_RQ_BASE 0x700` + 5, `VM_RQ_BASE 0xC00` + 5, `SEF_RQ_BASE 0xD00` + 2, `DS_RQ_BASE 0xE00` + 3, `SCHED_RQ_BASE 0xF00` + 4
- ordering `_Static_assert`s mirroring the Rust `const _` guards

Two notes to put in the header banner:
1. MINIX 3's `callnr.h` holds POSIX syscall numbers; ours holds kernel-call + server-request numbers (which MINIX keeps in `com.h`). Documented deviation.
2. `NR_KERN_CALLS_PHASE4` is emitted to C as **`NR_KERN_CALLS`** with `/* kernel-shared: NR_KERN_CALLS_PHASE4 */`. Baking a phase-scoped name into a header that freezes at 5.6 is a wart. Nominate the Rust-side rename for 5.1 (see risks).
3. **Payload byte offsets are not emitted in 5.0.** They exist only in Rust doc comments, not as constants, and D8's own scope says the headers grow as later slices add bands. Emit a comment saying so; the first musl wrapper (5.6) is the natural forcing function.

### 2.7 The errno artifact and the guarded-assertion scheme

This is the one place where the "compile it in CI" plan has a real hole, so it gets a real answer.

The problem: CI's syntax check runs with **no musl sysroot**, and the host `<errno.h>` has different values — on macOS `EDEADLK` is 11 and `EAGAIN` is 35, so a naive `_Static_assert(EDEADLK == 35)` against host headers is flatly wrong; on glibc it would happen to pass, i.e. pass for the wrong reason.

**Solution, three parts.**

**(a) `minix/errno.h` defines only the MINIX band**, never the POSIX block:

```c
#ifndef _MINIX_ERRNO_H
#define _MINIX_ERRNO_H
/*
 * Errno policy (phase-5 D7):
 *
 *   * The POSIX block (1..40) is deliberately NOT defined here. minix.rs
 *     adopts musl's numbering verbatim, so those values come from the C
 *     library's own <errno.h>, and musl's syscall_ret.c `r > -4096UL`
 *     convention works unmodified.
 *   * The MINIX-specific IPC errnos below live in modern MINIX 3's 200-band
 *     (sys/sys/errno.h), clear of Linux's entire range, so they can never
 *     collide with a musl-visible errno.
 *
 * Values here are POSITIVE magnitudes, matching <errno.h> convention. The
 * kernel and the Rust servers carry them NEGATED on the wire
 * (kernel-shared/src/error.rs); the musl wrapper negates on the way in.
 */
#define ENOTREADY    201
#define EDEADSRCDST  202
#define EDONTREPLY   203
#define EGENERIC     204
#define ELOCKED      208
#define EBADCALL     209
#define ECALLDENIED  210
#define ETRAPDENIED  211
#define EBADREQUEST  212
#define EBADSRCDST   216   /* modern MINIX spells this condition EBADEPT */

/* Always on, libc-independent: the band separation is what makes a collision
   with a musl errno impossible. */
_Static_assert(ENOTREADY  >= 200, "MINIX errno escaped the 200 band");
/* … one per name … */
```

**(b) The POSIX verification is opt-in and exact:**

```c
/*
 * Verification of the POSIX block against the C library actually being
 * compiled against. Opt-in because it is only meaningful against the musl
 * fork: CI's `clang -fsyntax-only` check runs with -nostdlibinc and no
 * sysroot, and a HOST <errno.h> uses different numbers (Darwin: EDEADLK 11,
 * EAGAIN 35), so asserting against it would be wrong -- or, on glibc, right
 * for the wrong reason. tools/build-musl.sh (slice 5.6) defines this macro so
 * the assertions fire against the fork's real bits/errno.h.
 */
#ifdef MINIX_ABI_CHECK_POSIX_ERRNO
#include <errno.h>
_Static_assert(EPERM == 1, "libc EPERM disagrees with kernel-shared error.rs");
/* … all 40, generated from error::ALL's POSIX block … */
#endif

#endif /* _MINIX_ERRNO_H */
```

**(c) A generated musl stand-in so CI still compiles the guarded block.** Without this, the 40 assertions inside `#ifdef` are never parsed until slice 5.6, and a typo (`EDEALDK`) sits undetected for six slices. The generator emits `abi-check/errno.h` — 40 `#define`s at musl's values, from the same `error::ALL` POSIX block, with a loud banner:

```c
/*
 * NOT A REAL LIBC HEADER. A stand-in so CI can compile <minix/errno.h>'s
 * guarded POSIX-verification block without a musl sysroot. It is generated
 * from the same Rust table the assertions check, so it is a SYNTAX and
 * NAME-SPELLING check, not a value check -- the real value check runs in
 * tools/build-musl.sh (slice 5.6) against the fork's bits/errno.h.
 * Never installed into a sysroot; reachable only via an explicit -I.
 */
```

Be honest about this in the plan doc: in 5.0 the errno artifact's value check is **deferred to 5.6**; what CI gains now is that the assertion text is valid C naming real macros.

### 2.8 `abi-selftest.c` — yes, emit it

A header is never a translation unit, so its `_Static_assert`s never fire and `-fsyntax-only` has nothing to chew on. The selftest is what makes the CI gate real.

```c
/* SPDX + GENERATED banner */
#include <minix/ipc.h>
#include <minix/com.h>
#include <minix/callnr.h>
#include <minix/errno.h>

/* A second time: proves every include guard actually guards. */
#include <minix/ipc.h>
#include <minix/com.h>
#include <minix/callnr.h>
#include <minix/errno.h>

/* Cross-header invariants no single header can express. */
_Static_assert(SCHED_RQ_BASE + NR_SCHED_MSGS - 1 < NOTIFY_MESSAGE, "…");
_Static_assert(PM_RQ_BASE > KERNEL_CALL + NR_KERN_CALLS - 1, "…");
_Static_assert(ANY != NONE && NONE != SELF && ANY != SELF, "…");
_Static_assert(SYSTEM_EP != HARDWARE_EP, "…");

/* ISO C forbids an empty translation unit. */
typedef int minixrs_abi_selftest_tu_;
```

(A file-scope unused typedef draws no warning; `-Wunused-local-typedef` is local-only.)

